course: dictionary
lesson: 13599
title: Using incorporate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To incorporate something means to include it as a part of a bigger thing. Think of it as taking something in and making it part of the whole. «We will incorporate our friends' ideas into the class story.» The ideas become part of the story, not a separate note.
explain: Incorporate can also mean to mix one thing into another. «Gently incorporate the eggs into the flour.» The eggs are stirred in until they blend with the flour. Ask yourself: is something being added so that it becomes one whole? If yes, incorporate may fit.
explain: In business and law, incorporate has a special meaning: to make a company into a legal organisation. You may see «Inc.» after some American company names, which means «Incorporated». This is a different meaning from the everyday ones, so check the sentence to see which one is used.
explain: Look at the clue words around it. Words like into, ideas or ingredients often sit near incorporate. Swap in the words include or mix in: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Incorporate is a verb: incorporate, incorporates, incorporated, incorporating.
fact: «Incorporate» means to include something as part of a bigger thing.
fact: «Incorporate» can also mean to mix one thing into another, as in «incorporate the butter into the flour».
fact: In business, «incorporated» means made into a legal organisation, as in «Inc.» after a company name.
fact: A good check is to swap in include or mix in and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The writer will incorporate her friend's idea into the story.» What does incorporate mean here? | Read the story out loud to the class | Draw a picture of the writer | *Include the idea as part of the story | Throw the idea away completely
exercise: «Gently incorporate the eggs into the flour.» What do you do to the eggs? | *Mix them in with the flour | Put them in the bin | Boil them in water | Leave them on the table
exercise: «Our company is now incorporated.» What does incorporated mean? | Lost in the post | Painted in bright colours | *Made into a legal organisation | Closed down for the holidays
test: «We will incorporate the chart into our science report.» What does incorporate mean? | Hide the chart from everyone | *Include the chart as part of the report | Delete the report | Send the report to the post office
test: Which word means to mix one thing gently into another? | *incorporate | hesitate | decorate | celebrate
exam: Ella says incorporate just means ignore. Is she right? | No, it means to fall asleep | Yes, it means to leave something out | Yes, it means to shout loudly | *No, it means to include something as part of a bigger thing
